Embedded React: keep transform sources across frames.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, in the patch order listed there.
Changed files: engine/CMakeLists.txt, engine/animation/animation.c,
engine/core/er_limits.h, engine/rendering/image_scaler.c,
engine/rendering/image_scaler.h, engine/rendering/transform.c,
engine/rendering/transform.h, engine/scene/compositor.c,
engine/scene/er_node_internal.h and engine/tests/rendering/CMakeLists.txt;
adds engine/tests/rendering/test_transform_cache.c.
Not proposed upstream: PS5 React maintains this change here.

A scaled or rotated node is painted by rendering its subtree into a transform
source and mapping that out. The capture ran on every frame of a scale
animation although only the matrix changed, and once in every parallel slice
the node straddled. On PS5, a focus scale on a 4K row card made each frame of
the move re-render the card in up to four slices: 30-43 ms of render per frame,
against 3 ms for the same move without the scale.

ERUI_XFORM_CACHE_SLOTS (default 0, compiled out) keeps that many 2D sources,
shared by every render worker. A source is keyed by the node, its size, the
image registry's generation and a new per-node content_stamp, which every
change to what the subtree paints advances along the ancestor chain. An
animated change to a node's own translate, scale or rotation leaves that
node's stamp alone. A hit walks the subtree for its paint bookkeeping only and
emits the kept pixels through the current matrix. A source is stored once the
same content has been captured twice, and only when it was captured whole.
Lookups and claims take the serial lock, and a slot used in the current commit
is never evicted while another worker may be reading it.

test_transform_cache records a scale animation with changing content twice,
with kept sources and with a fresh capture forced on every frame, and requires
every frame to match. It also checks that a kept source is really reused and
that re-registering an image invalidates it.

